2) How to create a table (basic syntax and examples of data types).
	
	"Tables are where data lives and its relationships are defined." pg. 22
	
	Open pgAdmin Query tool
	1 CREATE TABLE teachers (
		2 id bigserial
		3 first_name varchar(25),
		  last_name varchar(50)
		  school varchar(50)
		4 hire_date date,
		5 salary numeric
		6 );

	VARCHAR is the amount of storage space while (##) is character length maximum value

3) Why semicolons are important in SQL.

	"you end the statement with a semicolon, which signals the end of the command. You must end all PostgreSQL statements with a semicolon as part of the ANSI SQL standard." Sometimes you can get away with not ending with a semicolon, but it is not always the case so it is a good habit to form. Pg. 19
	

4) At least two examples of data types you might use in a table.
	
	varchar - which is character length
		first_name varchar(25),
		last_name varchar(50)
			
			stores variable length character text for teachers first and last names

	date - stores dates without attached times, date formatting is yyyy-mm-dd, this is international standard for date formats to help avoid confusion. Pg. 25
		
		an example of this is hire_date 2026-08-22

	numeric - can be used to store things like teachers salaries. salary numeric will help store teachers pay as a number value.


